# Transport Modes

## UDP (QUIC)

The default transport. Uses QUIC for native stream multiplexing and built-in TLS 1.3. Append `/udp` to the address, or omit the suffix entirely.

UDP is frequently blocked or rate-limited in corporate and cloud environments. If operating through a strict firewall or a proxy, use TCP instead.

Benchmarks at **~2.4 Gbps**. On high-loss or high-jitter networks, QUIC's per-stream loss recovery avoids head-of-line blocking that TCP transports are subject to.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/udp" /%}

## TCP (WebSockets)

TCP traffic is wrapped in WebSockets to appear as standard HTTPS to inspecting proxies, firewalls, and Deep Packet Inspection (DPI) tools. Use this transport when UDP is blocked or when blending into existing HTTPS traffic is required.

Append `/tcp` to the address.

Benchmarks at **~2.4 Gbps**. On low-loss networks, WebSocket offers ~21% lower average RTT than QUIC due to kernel-space TCP optimization.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/tcp" /%}

### CDN Fronting

With TLS enabled, WebSocket mode can be fronted through a CDN, making tunnel traffic appear as legitimate HTTPS to a known CDN domain. This adds an additional layer of obfuscation and makes the connection significantly harder to attribute or block.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/tcp --cert cert.pem --key key.pem" /%}
